==English==

===Etymology===
From {{inh|en|enm|imperturbable}}, from {{der|en|frm|imperturbable}} and directly from {{der|en|LL.|imperturbābilis}}, from {{m|la|im-}} + {{m|la|perturbō}} + {{m|la|-bilis}}. Surface analysis {{m|en|im-}} + {{m|en|perturbable}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{a|UK}} {{IPA|en|/ˌɪmpəˈtɜːbəbəl/}}
* {{a|US}} {{IPA|en|/ˌɪmpɚˈtɝbəbəl/}}

===Adjective===
{{en-adj}}

# Not easily [[perturb]]ed, [[upset]] or [[excited]].
#* {{RQ:Landon Ethel Churchill|page=280|volume=III|passage="We may keep it by us," replied the pawnbroker, "for months; there is no demand for such articles." "But," exclaimed she, eagerly, "I shall soon redeem it!" "So you all say," returned the man, with '''imperturbable''' coolness.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1962|month=August|author=G. Freeman Allen|title=Traffic control on the Great Northern Line|journal=Modern Railways|page=132|text=This sort of thing is meat and drink to the born Controller&mdash;and Controllers are born with the right '''imperturbable''' temperament for the job; hence the fact that they are recruited from many different grades of operating staff, and some recruits don't stay the course.}}
# [[calm|Calm]] and [[collected]], even under [[pressure]].

